    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d432">

                <pb facs="0609" n="323" sameAs="frus:frus1916Supp/pg323"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 851.857/40</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Chargé in Germany</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>)<anchor n="2" xml:id="d432-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-12-09T21:00:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">December 9, 1916, 9 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3656. Department advised that unarmed French ship <hi rendition="#italic">John
                        Lambert</hi>, Montreal to Havre with cargo of coal, was cannonaded without
                    notice by submarine of unknown nationality and sunk between Havre and Falmouth
                    November 22 last. Five or six Americans were among the crew.</p>
                <p>Please bring this case to notice of Foreign Minister with request for prompt
                    investigation and report.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d432-f1" n="2" type="source" xml:id="d432-f1-content">
                <p>The same to the Ambassador in Austria-Hungary, No. 1447.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
